  "body": [
    "With exchange-traded markets closed, the read rests on the week and month — and the month says the market is pricing a hot-nominal economy: growth holding up, inflation pressure building, and liquidity plentiful. The 10-year yield sits at 4.72%, 97% of its 52-week range, up 10 basis points on the month and 56 on the year, while copper trades at the very top of its own range, up 6% over the month and 18% year to date. Rising long yields alongside a strong industrial metal is a reflation signature, not a growth scare.",
    "The inflation-hedge leg is the tell. Gold rose 12% over the month even as yields climbed — gold rising with yields points to debasement and inflation pricing, not fear. Bitcoin did better still, up 22% on the month, and now buys 12% more gold than a month ago: risk appetite and liquidity, not flight to safety. A dollar down 1.6% over the month completes the picture and flatters every dollar-priced move.",
    "The tension is the copper/gold ratio, down 5% on the month as gold outran copper — a hint the bid is more monetary than industrial. But with copper at 97% of its range and Brent still up 45% year to date, the weight of evidence favors reflation with an inflationary tilt over anything defensive."
